// E2E tests for string literal escape sequences from the REPL (ADR 0138, BT-4165).
//
// Per-escape values are pinned by stdlib/test/string_escape_test.bt (BUnit)
// and the printString <-> re-parse legs by the shared conformance corpus
// (stdlib/test/fixtures/string_escape_corpus.json). This file proves the
// whole path works from a live REPL turn: lexing, evaluation, printString,
// and the unknown-escape compile error. Unknown-escape basics ("\q", "\d")
// live in errors.btscript (BT-4163); the error case here is the realistic
// Windows-path shape.

// ===========================================================================
// CRLF LITERAL
// ===========================================================================

// CRLF is one grapheme (ADR 0138 Grapheme unity)
"\r\n" size
// => 1

"a\r\nb" size
// => 3

// Byte-identical to the Character-built string
"\r\n" =:= ((Character value: 13) asString ++ (Character value: 10) asString)
// => true

// ===========================================================================
// PRINTSTRING ROUND-TRIP
// ===========================================================================

// printString escapes the newline: single-line, re-parseable spelling
"a\nb" printString
// => "a\nb"

// ... which is exactly the canonical spelling (the re-parse leg over the
// same spelling is pinned by the Rust conformance test on the shared corpus;
// this asserts the REPL shows that spelling)
("a\nb" printString) =:= """a\\nb"""
// => true

// ===========================================================================
// UNKNOWN ESCAPE IS A COMPILE ERROR
// ===========================================================================

// A backslash outside the table fails with a pointer and a \\ hint.
// Complete-but-invalid, so the REPL prints the error rather than showing a
// continuation prompt.
"C:\path"
// => ERROR: unknown escape
